FT                   /locus_tag="LOCUS_17910"
FT                   /note="WP_011267845.1 tryptophan-rich sensory protein
FT                   (Pseudomonas) [pid:93.8%, q_cov:100.0%, s_cov:100.0%,
FT                   Eval:4.5e-77]"
FT                   /note="Domain:cl01379:TSPO_MBR Translocator protein
FT                   (TSPO)/peripheral-type benzodiazepine receptor (MBR)
FT                   family. Tryptophan-rich sensory protein (TspO) is an
FT                   integral membrane protein that acts as a negative regulator
FT                   of the expression of specific photosynthesis genes in
FT                   response to oxygen/light. It is involved in the efflux of
FT                   porphyrin intermediates from the cell. This reduces the
FT                   activity of coproporphyrinogen III oxidase, which is
FT                   thought to lead to the accumulation of a putative repressor
FT                   molecule that inhibits the expression of specific
FT                   photosynthesis genes. Several conserved aromatic residues
FT                   are necessary for TspO function: they are thought to be
FT                   involved in binding porphyrin intermediates. In, the rat
FT                   mitochondrial peripheral benzodiazepine receptor (MBR) was
FT                   shown to not only retain its structure within a bacterial
FT                   outer membrane, but also to be able to functionally
FT                   substitute for TspO in TspO- mutants, and to act in a
FT                   similar manner to TspO in its in situ location: the outer
FT                   mitochondrial membrane. The biological significance of MBR
FT                   remains unclear, however. It is thought to be involved in a
FT                   variety of cellular functions, including cholesterol
FT                   transport in steroidogenic tissues. [Superfamily,
FT                   Aligned:25-146, Eval:3.7e-23, score:86.3]"
FT                   /note="MGA_1791"
