FT                   /note="Domain:cl25662:Lys2b Thioester reductase domain of
FT                   alpha aminoadipate reductase Lys2 and NRPSs [Secondary
FT                   metabolites biosynthesis, transport and catabolism]. This
FT                   model includes the terminal domain from the fungal alpha
FT                   aminoadipate reductase enzyme (also known as aminoadipate
FT                   semialdehyde dehydrogenase) which is involved in the
FT                   biosynthesis of lysine, as well as the reductase-containing
FT                   component of the myxochelin biosynthetic gene cluster,
FT                   MxcG. The mechanism of reduction involves activation of the
FT                   substrate by adenylation and transfer to a
FT                   covalently-linked pantetheine cofactor as a thioester. This
FT                   thioester is then reduced to give an aldehyde (thus
FT                   releasing the product) and a regenerated pantetheine thiol.
FT                   (In myxochelin biosynthesis this aldehyde is further
FT                   reduced to an alcohol or converted to an amine by an
FT                   aminotransferase.) This is a fundamentally different
FT                   reaction than beta-ketoreductase domains of polyketide
FT                   synthases which act at a carbonyl two carbons removed from
FT                   the thioester and forms an alcohol as a product. This
FT                   domain is invariably found at the C-terminus of the
FT                   proteins which contain it (presumably because it results in
FT                   the release of the product). The majority of hits to this
FT                   model are non-ribosomal peptide synthetases in which this
FT                   domain is similarly located proximal to a thiolation domain
FT                   (pfam00550). In some cases this domain is found at the end
FT                   of a polyketide synthetase enzyme, but is unlike
FT                   ketoreductase domains which are found before the thiolase
FT                   domains. Exceptions to this observed relationship with the
FT                   thiolase domain include three proteins which consist of
FT                   stand-alone reductase domains (GP|466833 from M. leprae,
FT                   GP|435954 from Anabaena and OMNI|NTL02SC1199 from Strep.
FT                   coelicolor) and one protein (OMNI|NTL01NS2636 from Nostoc)
FT                   which contains N-terminal homology with a small group of
FT                   hypothetical proteins but no evidence of a thiolation
FT                   domain next to the putative reductase domain. Below the
FT                   noise cutoff to this model are proteins containing more
FT                   distantly related ketoreductase and dehydratase/epimerase
FT                   domains. It has been suggested that a NADP-binding motif
FT                   can be found in the N-terminal portion of this domain that
FT                   may form a Rossman-type fold. [Superfamily,
FT                   Aligned:791-1084, Eval:3.1e-49, score:176.8, C-term
FT                   missing]"
